
:: arm_wrestler_start [broken_antler quest]
<<set $arm_wrestler_done = true>>
The noise started around the third round of terrible ale. Not the usual tavern noise   the ambient soup of conversation and coughing and furniture creaking that was the Broken Antler's default soundtrack. This was different. Focused. The concentrated buzz of people gathering around something interesting, which was remarkable because nothing interesting had happened in the Broken Antler since the founding of the monarchy and possibly before that.

I looked up from my tankard. The corner table   the big one, scarred and ring-stained, usually occupied by a rotating cast of sullen dock workers   had drawn a crowd. Six, seven people, pressing close, leaning in, the particular posture of spectators at a contest. Coin glinted on the table. Someone laughed. Someone else groaned.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Arm wrestling.
<</dialog>>

She said it the way she said most things   flatly, with just enough inflection to communicate that she'd already assessed the situation, found it beneath her, and was tolerating its existence in her visual field out of sheer generosity.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
How can you tell from here?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The rhythm. Listen. Crowd goes quiet   that's the grip. Low murmur   that's the push. Then either cheering or groaning   someone wins or loses. It's been cycling for the last twenty minutes. Someone's beating everyone.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/1.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I listened. She was right. The pattern was there, rhythmic as breathing   silence, murmur, eruption and the eruptions were mostly groans, which meant the same person was winning, which meant someone at that table had arms strong enough to beat every challenger in a working-class tavern, which was saying something because the Broken Antler's clientele was built from dock labor and disappointment, both of which produced considerable forearm development.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Don't even think about it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not thinking about anything.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're thinking about the exact thing I told you not to think about. I can see it. Your right arm just flexed.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That was involuntary.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your involuntary responses are the most honest thing about you, and they're all saying "I want to arm-wrestle that man."
<</dialog>>

She wasn't wrong. My right arm had flexed. The forearm, specifically   the thick cable of muscle running from elbow to wrist that seven years of daily swordwork had built into the most reliable piece of equipment I owned. It had flexed at the sound of competition the way a dog's ears prick at the sound of a whistle. Muscle memory. Reflex. The body saying yes before the brain had finished thinking maybe. I stood up.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Aldric 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm just going to look.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That's what you said about the dwarf in Kestwick.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I looked and then I arm-wrestled the dwarf. My shoulder clicked for a month.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You want to do this again because...?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Because I'm down to few coppers and there's coin on that table.
<</dialog>>

She opened her mouth. Closed it. The math was undeniable   we were broke, and the pile of silver glinting under the corner lamp was more money than we'd seen collectively in two weeks. Mira was pragmatic above all things, and pragmatism had a hard time arguing against eight silver when the alternative was porridge and misery.

<<dialog "Mira">>
If your shoulder clicks again, I'm not massaging it out this time.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You said that about Kestwick. You massaged it out three times.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I was in a generous mood. I'm not in a generous mood.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're never in a generous mood.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Yet here I am. Following you toward another bad decision. There's probably a lesson in that.
<</dialog>>

[[Go towards table|arm_wrestler_brokk]]


:: arm_wrestler_brokk [broken_antler quest]
<<set $arm_wrestler_done = true>>
<<removesidequest "arm_wrestler">>
The crowd parted for me   the way crowds always parted for a man with a sword on his hip, even in a place where swords were common currency. Not out of respect. Out of the instinct that said this one might be next and I want a good viewing angle and then I saw Brokk.

He sat at the corner table like a mountain sits in a valley   immovably, inevitably, with the casual certainty of a geological feature that had been there since before the surrounding landscape was relevant. Early twenties. Built like someone had taken a normal human body and hired a second crew to add more of everything. Arms like bridge cables. Shoulders that could have supported a load-bearing wall. A neck that started at his jaw and ended at his collarbones without the usual courtesy of narrowing in between. His hands   gods, his hands   were the size of dinner plates, each finger thick as a sausage, the palms calloused to the texture of old leather.

He was a blacksmith's apprentice. I didn't need to be told   the burns on his forearms said it, the soot embedded in his knuckle creases said it, the particular development of his deltoids and triceps said it. Forge work. Hammer work. The monotonous, beautiful brutality of heating metal and hitting it until it became something useful. His body was the biography of his trade, written in muscle and scar tissue.

He'd beaten four challengers. They sat around the table in various states of defeat   nursing ales, flexing sore wrists, wearing the particular expression of men whose masculinity had been readjusted by a man half their age. The pot on the table had grown to eight silver in mixed coin, contributed by the losers and augmented by side bets from the crowd.

Brokk saw me looking. He grinned. Not a smirk   a genuine, uncomplicated grin. The grin of a young man who was very good at one specific thing and took pure, uncynical pleasure in being good at it. No malice. No posturing. Just the simple delight of a body doing what it was built for.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/2.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Brokk">>
You look like you've got arms under that tunic. Want to test them?
<</dialog>>

His voice was surprisingly light for his size   a clear, carrying tenor that didn't match the bass rumble you'd expect from a man built like a fortification. Young. Cheerful. The voice of someone who hadn't yet learned that the world was going to ask more from him than physical strength, and who wouldn't believe it if you told him.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What's the buy-in?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Two silver to sit. Winner takes the pot. Eight silver and change   more than you'd make in a week of honest work, assuming you do honest work, which with that sword I'm guessing you don't.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I do very honest work. People pay me to solve problems.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
What of the sword?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Some problems don't respond to polite conversation.
<</dialog>>

Brokk laughed. A big, room-filling laugh, the kind that came from a chest that could have served as a resonating chamber for a cathedral bell. Several people in the crowd smiled reflexively   laughter that genuine was contagious, a biological response to unfiltered joy.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
I like you. Sit down, sellsword. Let's see what you've got.
<</dialog>>

Mira materialized at my shoulder. I hadn't heard her approach   I never did, half-elf silence being what it was   but I felt her presence the way you feel a change in the weather. A hand on my arm. Light. Fingers barely touching the fabric.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Two silver is everything we have left after the room.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I know.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If you lose, we can't eat.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Then I'd better not lose.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That's not a strategy. That's an aspiration.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
All my strategies are aspirations. It's worked out so far.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It has emphatically not worked out so far. We are in this tavern with few coppers because your previous aspirations brought us here. Your aspiration history is a litany of 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Eight silver, Mira. Eight silver and a free ale. When was the last time we had eight silver?
<</dialog>>

She went quiet. The amber eyes did the math   the math she always did, the survival arithmetic that ran underneath everything like a river under cobblestones. Nine coppers. A room paid through one more day. A city that charged three coppers for ale that tasted like grief and on the table, gleaming under lamplight, eight silver coins that represented a month of meals and the breathing room they hadn't had since Thornwall.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your shoulder.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Is fine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If it clicks 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It won't click.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If it clicks, you're useless for a week. We have a contract from Grent. A mine to clear. You need your sword arm.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My sword arm is my right. I'll wrestle left.
<</dialog>>

She stared at me. I stared back. The standoff of two people who'd been arguing with each other for three months and had developed the particular efficiency of combatants who knew each other's moves.


[[Challenge Brokk|arm_wrestler_challenge]]
[[Decline|arm_wrestler_decline]]
