:: arm_wrestler_challenge [broken_antler quest]

I sat down. The chair across from Brokk was warm   body heat from the four men who'd sat there before me and lost. The wood of the table was scarred with decades of abuse, the surface a topography of ring-stains and knife-marks and the divots left by countless fists, tankards, and foreheads being driven into it by force or drink.

I counted out two silver from my belt pouch and placed them on the table. They joined the pot with a pair of soft clicks that sounded, to my money-starved ears, like a door closing. No going back. Those coins were in the pot now, and they were coming out in my pocket or someone else's.

Brokk planted his elbow. The motion was practiced   a smooth, mechanical deployment of arm and shoulder onto the table surface, the elbow finding its divot in the wood (he'd been sitting here long enough to wear a depression), the forearm rising vertical, the hand opening like a gate. I planted mine.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/3.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Good arm. Sword arm?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Both arms. I switch hands depending on the fight.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Fancy. I just hit things until they're the right shape.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Swords or steel?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Both. Though the steel complains less.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/4.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I gripped his hand. The contact was immediate and informative, the way shaking a stranger's hand always tells you something but this told you everything. His palm was a slab   callused to the texture of boiled leather, hot, dry, the skin so thick over the knuckles that it barely registered pressure. His fingers closed around mine like iron bands   not squeezing, not yet, but present. The grip of a man whose hands had been closing around the handles of heavy things since before his voice broke. He could crush walnuts in that grip. He could crush bone.

My grip answered. Different vocabulary, same language. Sword-calluses rather than forge-calluses   the pads and ridges that formed along the palm and fingers from thousands of hours of gripping a leather-wrapped hilt. My forearm was thick but not massive   built for endurance and control rather than raw output, the kind of forearm that could swing a longsword for an hour without cramping because the muscles had been trained to work efficiently rather than explosively.

The crowd pressed closer. Bodies around us, warm and ale-scented, the circle tightening like a noose made of spectators. Someone was taking side bets. Someone else was providing commentary in a low, rapid mutter that sounded like an auctioneer describing a horse race.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Ready?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Go.
<</dialog>>

He pushed. The force was   there was no other word   staggering. Not a burst. Not an explosion. A wave. A slow, mounting, inexorable pressure that came from his shoulder through his arm through his hand through mine and into every tendon, every joint, every fiber of muscle from my wrist to my spine. The man didn't push the way fighters pushed   with speed and aggression and the psychology of dominance. He pushed the way gravity pushed. Constantly. Inevitably. Without any interest in whether you were ready for it.

My arm bent. One inch. Two. The tendons in my forearm stood out like suspension cables, the veins distending, the skin going white over the knuckles where my grip clamped down in desperate resistance. My shoulder   the same shoulder that had clicked for a month after the dwarf in Kestwick   screamed a warning that my brain acknowledged and my pride overruled.

The first five seconds were assessment. Reading him through the contact   the way you read a blade's quality through the vibration of a parry. His strength was massive, unrefined, a raw material that had never been processed into technique. He pushed in a straight line because a straight line was all he'd ever needed. Nobody in this tavern had forced him to do anything else.

I shifted my grip. Slightly. A millimeter of repositioning   the wrist angling inward, changing the vector, redirecting his force from a direct contest of power into a lever equation where his mass became a liability and my angle became an advantage. A sword technique. The same principle that let a longsword redirect a warhammer's blow   you didn't match force, you channeled it.

Brokk's eyebrows rose. His arm, which had been pressing mine steadily toward the table, stopped. Not because I'd overpowered him   I hadn't, not remotely   but because the angle had changed and his straight-line push was suddenly meeting resistance on a diagonal that his muscle memory didn't know how to solve.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Oh. That's clever.
<</dialog>>

He adjusted. Pushed harder. Not just his arm now but his shoulder, his chest, the rotational force of his entire upper body leaning into the contest. The table creaked under the pressure. My arm shook   a visible tremor running from elbow to wrist, the muscles fiber-firing in rapid, desperate pulses. The scar on my ribcage pulled tight, the tissue complaining at the strain. My shoulder was making sounds   internal sounds, the grinding complaints of a joint being asked to perform at the edge of its tolerance.

The crowd had gone silent. The particular silence of people watching something balance on a knife's edge   not breathing, not moving, the collective unconscious understanding that the outcome was being decided in this exact second and anything could tip it.

I was losing. The math was clear. Brokk was stronger. Objectively, measurably, inarguably stronger   his arm was the product of genetics and daily forge work and the simple, unfair advantage of a body built for raw power competing against a body built for endurance. My technique had bought me time. It hadn't bought me victory.

My arm was three inches from the table. Two inches. I could feel the wood's proximity like heat   the finish line of failure, waiting for my knuckles.

I thought about the mine. The contract. The twenty silver that Grent's job would pay. I thought about Mira, watching from behind the crowd, her amber eyes tracking the contest with the precision she applied to everything. I thought about the few coppers in my belt pouch that were all that stood between us and the fish-gutting docks and I thought about my arm. This arm. The thick forearm, the calloused hand, the shoulder that could swing a longsword for an hour, the grip that could hold a blade in rain and blood and fear. My arm. My body. The machine I'd been operating for twenty-six years, the only tool I'd ever truly owned.

Something surged. Not technique this time. Not strategy. Just   everything. The last reserves. The bottom of the barrel. The place where the body stores the power it doesn't want to use because using it costs something you can't get back. It came from my chest, my gut, the base of my spine, traveling up through the shoulder and down the arm and into the hand and through the grip like lightning through a rod. I pushed.

Brokk's arm stopped falling. Held. Quivered at the balance point   neither going down nor coming up, both of us locked in a trembling equilibrium that the crowd absorbed with a collective intake of breath.

Then, slowly   inch by inch, tendon by screaming tendon   I pushed him back. His knuckles touched the table with a crack that I felt in my teeth. The crowd erupted.

[[Pushed him back|arm_wrestler_victory]]



:: arm_wrestler_victory [broken_antler quest]

Cheering. Shouting. Someone banging a tankard against the table in rhythmic celebration. The particular, unrestrained roar of a working-class crowd watching an underdog win   because that's what I was, apparently, the lean swordsman against the blacksmith mountain, and the lean swordsman had planted the mountain's hand in the wood and the crowd was drunk enough and happy enough to treat it like a minor miracle.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/5.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I released Brokk's hand. My own was shaking   a deep, bone-level tremor that started in the fingers and ran all the way up to the shoulder, the aftershock of muscles that had been asked for everything and had delivered and were now presenting the bill. My forearm felt like someone had run it through a mangle. My shoulder was making clicking sounds. Small ones. Promising to become bigger ones.

Brokk leaned back in his chair. He looked at his own hand   the hand that had been driven into the table   with the expression of a man examining an old friend who'd just done something unexpected. Then he laughed.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Ha! That was   that grip change, the angle, right in the middle   you fight dirty, sellsword!
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I fight smart.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Same thing with better posture. Here 
<</dialog>>

He reached across the table and pushed the entire pot toward me. Eight silver and change   the change being a handful of coppers that the side-bettors had contributed. It sat in front of me, glinting under the lamplight, more money than I'd held in one place since Thornwall.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
You earned it. That's the best match I've had in weeks. Most of these lot 
<</dialog>>

He waved dismissively at his previous opponents, who had the good grace to look embarrassed.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
 they just push. Straight push, no thinking, like they're trying to shove a door open. You actually thought. Mid-match. While my arm was trying to break yours off. That takes something.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Desperation, mostly.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Best fuel there is. Let me buy you an ale. Consider it the loser's tax.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/6.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

He signaled the barmaid   Tess, the freckled one, who arrived with two tankards and a grin that said she'd been watching the whole thing and approved   and we drank. Bad ale. The worst ale. But it tasted different when someone else was buying it. Everything tasted different when you had eight silver in your pocket and a crowd of strangers treating you like you'd done something worth cheering for.

Brokk was good company. Simple, in the best sense of the word   a man without subtext, without calculation, without the layers of motive and strategy that made most human interaction a negotiation. He talked about the forge, about his master (a man named Halgrim who apparently communicated primarily through grunts and the occasional thrown hammer), about the satisfaction of heating iron until it glowed and then shaping it into something useful. He talked with his hands   big, expressive gestures that nearly knocked over both tankards and once came close to concussing a bystander.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
It's the best feeling in the world, honestly. Taking raw metal   ugly, shapeless, nothing   and turning it into something with purpose. A hinge. A blade. A horseshoe. Doesn't matter what. The transformation is the thing. Before, it's nothing. After, it's something and your hands did that.
<</dialog>>

He flexed his enormous fingers. Studied them with the simple affection of a craftsman admiring his own tools.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
These hands make things. That's their job. That's what they're for. Everything else   arm wrestling, lifting, breaking   that's just the side benefit of having hands that were built to shape the world.
<</dialog>>

I looked at my own hands. Wrapped around a tankard. Calloused, scarred, thick-fingered. Hands that had held swords and throats and reins and women and the occasional dying man. Hands that broke things rather than built them. The side benefit of a body that was shaped by the world rather than shaping it. Good hands. Strong hands. My hands.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You ever worry about losing them? The strength, I mean. Injury, age, something that takes the forge away from you.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Every blacksmith worries about that. One bad burn. One dropped hammer on the wrong finger. One morning where the grip doesn't close the way it used to. It's the nightmare. You wake up and your hands are different and you can't do the thing that makes you you.
<</dialog>>

He said it lightly. Philosophically. The hypothetical fear of a twenty-two-year-old whose hands were still perfect and who couldn't truly imagine them being otherwise.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
But that's why you use them while you've got them, right? Every match, every forge day, every time these things close around something and hold   it's a gift. Doesn't last forever. Nothing does. So you grip hard while you can and you don't waste the good years being afraid of the bad ones.
<</dialog>>

He raised his tankard. I raised mine. We drank. I thought about gripping hard while I could. About hands that might change. About the bad years that might not be years at all but something stranger and closer and less predictable than age or injury.

I didn't know yet. I didn't know about the potion or the healer or the vial waiting for me in a cellar that I hadn't found yet. I didn't know that these hands   the ones gripping this tankard, the ones that had just beaten a blacksmith in a contest of raw strength   were on a countdown.

I just drank my ale and enjoyed the eight silver and the company of a man who made things for a living and who'd told me, without knowing he was telling me, the only philosophy that mattered, Grip hard while you can.

[[Go back to Mira|arm_wrestler_mira_beat]]




:: arm_wrestler_mira_beat [broken_antler quest]

Mira was in the booth when I got back. She'd watched the whole thing   of course she had. Archer's eyes. She'd probably tracked every muscle twitch, every angle shift, every moment where the match could have gone either way.

She was picking her teeth with an arrowhead. The broadhead tip clicking against her canines. The sound that meant she was thinking about something and wasn't ready to share it.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/7.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

I dropped the eight silver on the table between us. They rang against the wood   the sound of not being broke anymore, at least for a week.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your shoulder.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Fine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It's clicking. I can hear it from here.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's settling. It does that.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It does that because you keep doing things that make it click. Like arm-wrestling blacksmiths. In taverns. For eight silver.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Eight silver and change.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
The change doesn't offset the medical expenses if your shoulder gives out before the mine job.
<</dialog>>

She was scolding me. Mira scolded the way she did everything   efficiently, sharply, with complete conviction and zero expectation that it would change my behavior. The scolding was a formality. A ritual. She scolded, I ignored the scolding, and we both pretended this wasn't a pattern that had been running since Thornwall.

But she was looking at the eight silver with the expression she reserved for unexpected good fortune   not quite a smile, but a softening of the hard lines around her mouth. A release of tension she'd been carrying since our coin had dropped to single digits.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your forearm. The thing you did with the grip   the angle shift, mid-press. That was a parry redirect.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You saw that?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I see everything. You took a first-position longsword parry and translated it into a biomechanical redirect. You used his force vector against his own line of pressure. That's not arm wrestling. That's swordwork.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It worked.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
It worked because you're clever with your body. You always have been. You don't have the raw strength for these contests   not really. But you have the intelligence to make your strength count for more than it should. It's why you're a good fighter. You think while you fight. Most swordsmen just swing.
<</dialog>>

She said it casually. A throwaway observation, delivered between arrowhead clicks. But I heard what was underneath it   the thing Mira would never say directly because direct statements of admiration violated her personal code of emotional austerity. She was proud of me.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You could have taken him.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Excuse me?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Brokk. You could have beaten him. Half-elf muscle density   you're stronger pound-for-pound than any human in this room.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know I could have beaten him.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Then why didn't you?
<</dialog>>

She stopped clicking the arrowhead. Looked at me. The amber eyes, lamp-lit, warm-gold in the dim tavern glow. Something moved across her face   not amusement, not exactly. Something more private. More tender. The expression she wore in those rare moments when the fortress walls came down and the woman behind them was visible.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Because I wanted to watch you do it.
<</dialog>>

She said it simply. Without embellishment. The way she'd state a fact about weather or terrain   a thing that was true and didn't require decoration.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You   your arm. When you pushed. That last surge. Your whole body committed. Every muscle, every tendon, your teeth were clenched and your neck was corded and your shoulders were wide, Aldric, wider than they've ever been because everything was engaged and everything was working and you looked 
<</dialog>>

She stopped. Pressed her lips together. The fortress walls slamming back into place, the drawbridge rising, the moment of vulnerability locked away behind thirty-eight years of emotional fortification.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You looked strong. That's all.
<</dialog>>

That wasn't all. I could hear the words she'd swallowed   the ones that lived in the space between you looked strong and whatever she'd actually been about to say. The words that would have crossed a line we'd agreed, silently, not to cross. Not yet. Not in a tavern. Not while we were still pretending that what happened between us in the dark was different from what existed between us in the light.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Strong enough to buy you an ale?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Strong enough to buy me two. My emotional support fee.
<</dialog>>

I bought her two. We sat in the corner booth of the worst tavern in Valdmere and drank terrible ale paid for by eight silver won from a blacksmith's apprentice in a contest of strength, and for a span of time that I didn't bother measuring, the world was small and warm and exactly the right shape.

Her foot found mine under the table. Pressed against it. Stayed. Neither of us mentioned it.

[[Drink ale|broken_antler]]
