> Brokk: Potential recurring NPC. Friendly, respects Aldric. When Aldric returns mid-transformation, Brokk might be the first person in Valdmere to recognize the eyes and the smile in a completely different body. / */



:: arm_wrestler_decline [broken_antler quest]

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Not tonight.
<</dialog>>

The words came out steady, practical, sensible. Mira's words in my voice. The pragmatic choice. The smart choice. The choice that preserved my shoulder for the mine contract and kept our two silver safely in my pouch rather than gambled on a table against a man whose arms were wider than my thighs. Brokk shrugged   a massive, amiable gesture.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
No shame in knowing your limits. Different kind of strength, that.
<</dialog>>

He turned to the crowd, looking for the next challenger. The noise swelled again   the cycle continuing, the contest rolling on without me. The eight silver stayed on the table, glinting under the lamplight, belonging to someone who wasn't me. I went back to the booth. Mira looked up from her arrowhead.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Smart.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/8.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Boring.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Smart and boring are the same thing. That's what makes boring people live longer.
<</dialog>>

She slid my tankard across the table. The terrible ale. The few coppers still in my pouch. The contract waiting. The mine waiting. Everything in its right place, nothing risked, nothing gained.

I drank. The ale tasted like regret. But regret was cheaper than a dislocated shoulder, and we had a job to do.

[[Drink Ale|broken_antler]]
/* / > [END   OPTION B: DECLINE]
>
> No coin gained. No relationship beat with Brokk.
> Mira approves the pragmatic choice. The eight silver stays unclaimed.
> Thematic note: The road not taken. Aldric chose preservation over performance. Later, when the transformation makes such contests impossible, the memory of the choice he didn't make carries a different weight   not regret for losing, but regret for not playing. / */


:: arm_wrestler_send_mira [broken_antler quest]

I looked at Brokk. Looked at Mira. Looked at Brokk's arms, which were the approximate diameter of most people's legs. Looked at Mira's arms, which were lean and wiry and contained, beneath the copper-toned skin, the densest muscle tissue evolution had ever produced in a humanoid species.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Mira. You want those eight silver?
<</dialog>>

She raised an eyebrow. The left one. The expression that meant I know exactly what you're suggesting and I'm deciding whether to be offended or intrigued.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You want me to arm-wrestle the man built like a cathedral.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Half-elf muscle density. Pound for pound, you're stronger than anyone in this room. Possibly stronger than him.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I am stronger than him. That doesn't mean I want to prove it in a tavern full of drunk men who'll either leer at me or cry about it afterward.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/9.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Eight silver.
<</dialog>>

She looked at the pot. The calculation behind her eyes was visible   not just the financial math but the social math, the identity math, the particular computation of a half-elf woman deciding whether to reveal a capability she usually kept hidden in a room full of humans who already looked at her like she was something other. The pointed ears. The amber eyes. The body that aged at half the human rate and stored its strength in fibers so dense they registered as something between flesh and cable.

She usually hid it. Not out of shame   Mira didn't do shame   but out of strategy. The less people knew about what she could do, the more advantage she had when it mattered. Revealing her strength for eight silver in a tavern was the opposite of strategic. But eight silver was eight silver.

[[Mira accepts|arm_wrestler_mira_accept]]

:: arm_wrestler_mira_accept [broken_antler quest]

<<dialog "Mira">>
Fine. But I'm doing this under protest and you're buying my ale for the rest of the week.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
With the eight silver we're about to win, I'll buy your ale for the rest of the month.
<</dialog>>

She stood. Pulled her vest straight. Rolled her sleeves up   a deliberate, theatrical gesture that she'd never admit was theatrical but absolutely was. Her forearms were revealed: lean, tawny, corded with tendons that shifted under the skin like rigging on a ship. Not impressive by human standards. Not thick or bulging or any of the things the crowd associated with arm-wrestling prowess. Just... precise. Clean-lined. The forearms of someone whose muscles had been engineered for a different operating standard.

She walked to the table. The crowd parted   differently than they'd parted for me. Curious. Amused. The particular parting of a crowd that expects entertainment rather than competition.

Brokk saw her coming. His grin widened   not cruelly, not with the mean condescension of a man who looked down on women. With genuine, uncomplicated amusement. The grin of someone who thought this was going to be fun.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Now here's something new. Welcome, welcome. Two silver to sit, same as everyone.
<</dialog>>

Mira placed two silver on the table   my two silver, technically, since she'd taken them from my pouch without asking on the walk over. Typical. She sat down. Planted her elbow. Opened her hand.

Brokk looked at her hand. Looked at his. The size difference was comic   her fingers were long, strong, but slender, and his were the approximate dimensions of large root vegetables. He could have closed his hand around hers twice.

He gripped. She gripped back. His eyebrows lifted   a fraction, barely perceptible, the first data point registering. Her grip wasn't what he'd expected. The fingers that looked slender had closed around his with a pressure that was... significant.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/10.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Ready?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Go.
<</dialog>>

[[They Start|arm_wrestler_mira_start]]

:: arm_wrestler_mira_start [broken_antler quest]

He pushed. Mira didn't move. Not barely didn't move. Not strained against it and held. Didn't move. Her arm absorbed his initial push the way a bowstring absorbs the tension of a draw   bending slightly at the wrist, storing the energy, the muscles in her forearm engaging with a smooth, mechanical efficiency that had nothing in common with the grunting, veining, brute-force struggles the crowd had been watching all evening.

Brokk's grin faltered. He pushed harder. Mira held.

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Well now.
<</dialog>>

He committed. Shoulder. Chest. The full rotational force that had driven four men's hands into the table in under ten seconds. The table creaked. Mira's arm trembled   the first sign of strain, a fine vibration running from wrist to elbow. But she held. Not by matching his force   she couldn't, not in absolute terms. By managing it. Channeling it. Letting it flow through her arm the way a river flows around a stone   not stopped, but redirected, dissipated, neutralized by angles and density and a muscle architecture that operated on fundamentally different engineering principles than his.

Half-elf physiology. Leaner. Denser. Muscle fibers packed tighter, organized more efficiently, the evolutionary product of a species that had spent millennia in forests where mass was a liability and strength-to-weight ratio was survival. What Brokk was pushing against wasn't a woman's arm. It was a mechanism. A system. A cable-and-pulley arrangement wrapped in copper-skinned packaging that looked fragile and was anything but.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/11.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

The crowd had stopped laughing. The amusement had drained out of the room like water from a cracked barrel, replaced by something else   the dawning, uncomfortable recognition that something unexpected was happening. The woman was holding. The woman was holding. Against Brokk. Against the man who'd beaten four challengers without breaking a sweat. Then Mira fired.

The push came from her back. From the lats   the broad, flat muscles that archers developed over decades of drawing bowstrings, the muscles that most people didn't even know they had but that Mira had trained into weapons. The force traveled from her spine through her shoulder through her arm through her hand and into Brokk's wrist with the clean, decisive transfer of a bowstring releasing. His knuckles hit the table in under ten seconds.

The sound the tavern made was the most beautiful thing I'd heard in weeks. A collective gasp   a full-body, room-wide intake of breath, the sound of two dozen people's assumptions being rearranged by reality. Then silence. One heartbeat. Two. Then the place exploded.

Cheering. Howling. Someone banging a tankard on a table so hard that the tankard broke and ale sprayed across three spectators who didn't care because they were too busy shouting. A dock worker in the back threw his hat in the air. The barmaid   Tess   was laughing with her hands over her mouth, her eyes wide and bright and delighted.

Brokk stared at his own hand. Turned it over. Examined the knuckles, the palm, the wrist, as if checking for structural damage that might explain how a woman a foot shorter and a hundred pounds lighter had just planted it in the wood. He found no explanation. His hand was intact. His pride was adjusting. Then he laughed.

The same room-filling laugh as before   genuine, delighted, without a trace of bitterness. The laugh of a man whose ego was robust enough to survive defeat and whose spirit was generous enough to appreciate the force that had inflicted it.

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/12.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

<<dialog "Brokk">>
HA! By the forge   what are you?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Half-elf.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Half-elf, half-siege-engine. Gods. That was   where did that come from? Your arm didn't even 
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Different muscle structure. Denser fibers. Higher strength-to-weight ratio and thirty-eight years of drawing a bowstring that requires seventy pounds of pull force at full draw.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Brokk">>
Seventy  that's more than my forge bellows!
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your forge bellows don't need to kill things at two hundred paces.
<</dialog>>

She scooped up the ten silver   the original eight plus our two back   with a motion so smooth and practiced it was almost invisible. Coins vanishing from table to hand to pouch like a magic trick. She stood, nodded to Brokk with the curt, professional acknowledgment of one athlete to another, and walked back to the booth.

[[Mira comes to me|arm_wrestler_mira_beat_1]]

:: arm_wrestler_mira_beat_1

She dropped the coins on the table in front of me. They rang against the wood.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your ale money. For the month, I believe you said.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That was... extraordinary.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
That was leverage. Literally. He pushed in a straight line. I redirected. Same thing you'd have done with a sword if anyone had ever taught you subtlety.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You beat a blacksmith in under ten seconds.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Eight. I counted.
<</dialog>>

<img src="img/scene/arm_wrestler/13.png" style="width: 100%; max-width: 768px; margin: 0 auto;" />

She picked up her arrowhead. Started clicking it against her canine. The rhythm steady, confident, the metronome of a woman who'd just proven something and was already moving on to thinking about the next thing.

I watched her. The lean arms, back to their usual resting state   unimpressive, unremarkable, the secret hidden again beneath copper skin and a half-elf's deceptive slightness. The confidence. The certainty. The way she inhabited her body with the absolute, unquestioning trust of a person whose physical self had never surprised her, never betrayed her, never woken her up in the night with the suspicion that something was changing without permission.

She knew what she was. Every muscle, every capability, every limit. Her body was an instrument she'd spent thirty-eight years tuning, and it played exactly the notes she asked for, every time, without exception.

I envied that. Not her strength   not really. Her certainty. The unshakable knowledge of what you were and what you could do.

I didn't know it then   sitting in that booth, counting silver, watching her teeth click against the arrowhead   but that certainty was the thing I'd lose first. Before the muscles. Before the height. Before the jaw and the voice and the hands. The certainty would go. The trust between a person and their body, the baseline faith that tomorrow you'd wake up in the same machine you went to sleep in. That would dissolve before anything visible changed.

But I didn't know that yet. I just envied her, without knowing what I was envying, and drank my ale, and was happy.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You could have let me try first.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You'd have lost. Your technique is good but his mass advantage was too high. He'd have beaten you in twenty seconds, you'd have injured your shoulder, and we'd have been out two silver instead of up eight. My way was faster, cheaper, and didn't require a recovery period.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You don't know I'd have lost.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I know your arm. Intimately.
<</dialog>>

She said intimately without inflection   flat, factual, the word carrying none of its secondary meanings despite the fact that she'd gripped that arm in bed, dug her nails into that forearm, felt those muscles flex under her fingers in contexts that had nothing to do with arm wrestling. Or maybe all of its secondary meanings. With Mira, you could never tell.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You enjoyed that.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I enjoyed winning. Winning is enjoyable. That's why people do it.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You enjoyed proving something.
<</dialog>>

She stopped clicking the arrowhead. Looked at me. The amber eyes steady, unblinking.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I enjoyed being underestimated. There's a particular pleasure in it   walking into a room where everyone assumes you're the lesser, and then demonstrating, with complete finality, that their assumptions were the only weak thing present.
<</dialog>>

She went back to the arrowhead. Click. Click. Click.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You should try it sometime.
<</dialog>>

She didn't elaborate. I didn't ask. The evening folded around us   tavern noise, ale warmth, the clink of our hard-won silver   and somewhere in the room behind us, Brokk was already challenging his next opponent, the contest rolling on, the cycle of strength and defeat continuing with or without us.

My foot found hers under the table. Pressed. She pressed back.

